  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing": "Dynamic Pricing",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.defaultVolatility": "Default Volatility",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.defaultVolatility.desc": "Size of the random daily price wobble. 0.1 makes each item wander within about plus or minus 10% of its demand-based value and drift back toward the middle every day. 0 disables the wobble. Each item's own volatility multiplies this, so an item with volatility 0 never wobbles.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.demandHalfLifeDays": "Demand Half Life Days",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.demandHalfLifeDays.desc": "How long SoftResponse mode remembers purchases, in game days. A purchase still counts for 50% after this many days, 25% after twice as long, and so on. Higher values let demand build up over a longer stretch and make prices recover more slowly. SoftResponse only.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.incrementalLongHalfLifeDays": "Incremental Long Half Life Days",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.incrementalLongHalfLifeDays.desc": "Incremental mode's \"normal\" buying-rate window in game days, used as the baseline. Only buying above this baseline raises the price. Higher values make the baseline adapt more slowly, so a lasting increase in buying keeps prices high for longer before they settle. Incremental only.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.incrementalScaleK": "Incremental Scale K",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.incrementalScaleK.desc": "The excess buying rate (items per day above the baseline) that pushes the price halfway to the cap in Incremental mode, i.e. half of (maxDemandFactor - 1) times the base price. Smaller values make small rushes swing the price a lot; larger values mean only big rushes move it.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.incrementalShortHalfLifeDays": "Incremental Short Half Life Days",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.incrementalShortHalfLifeDays.desc": "Incremental mode's \"current\" buying-rate window in game days. Short values (1-2) make the price react quickly to today's buying. Incremental only.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.maxDemandFactor": "Max Demand Factor",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.maxDemandFactor.desc": "The highest demand-driven multiplier in Incremental mode. 10 means a full rush can lift a price toward 10x its base (it approaches but never quite reaches it). Incremental only.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.maxMultiplier": "Max Multiplier",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.maxMultiplier.desc": "The highest the final price can go, as a multiple of the item's base price. 11 means no item can ever cost more than 11x base. This is the hard cap that also limits SoftResponse mode when demand is enormous.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.minMultiplier": "Min Multiplier",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.minMultiplier.desc": "The lowest the final price can go, as a fraction of the item's base price (defined in the market data pack). 0.8 means prices never fall below 80% of base.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.mode": "Mode",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.mode.desc": "How the market reacts to buying. Disabled - every item always costs its base price; no random fluctuation and no demand effect. SoftResponse - any buying slowly raises the price on a logarithmic curve, so the more that is bought the higher it goes, but even with many players it stays well below the cap and returns to base when buying stops. Incremental - the price rises only while buying is above the recent norm (a rush or new players) and settles back to base once buying returns to normal. Requires marketStats to be true.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.softDemandScale": "Soft Demand Scale",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.softDemandScale.desc": "The demand at which SoftResponse starts to noticeably raise the price (demand is roughly the number of items bought, weighted by demandHalfLifeDays). Smaller values let even modest buying move the price; larger values mean only heavy, sustained buying does.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.softResponseStrength": "Soft Response Strength",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.dynamicPricing.softResponseStrength.desc": "How strongly SoftResponse mode reacts to demand: the multiplier is 1 + strength x ln(1 + demand / scale). Higher means the same amount of buying raises prices more. With the default 0.4, demand equal to the scale raises the price by about 28%.",
  "fruitfulfun.config.market.marketStats": "Market Stats",
